"fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":7,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I've written the design and constraints for adding `round_currency` to `src/app_int/main.py`. It covers all 12 acceptance criteria.\n\n**Not run:** the shell command that validates `constraints.json` and checks the rounding approach against the criteria values was blocked. I checked both by hand instead. The JSON has only a `constraints` list, and each entry has just `id` and `description`. I worked through every expected value in AC-2 to AC-8 manually with the planned approach, and they all match.\n\n**Rounding mode:** your issue asked to be consulted rather than have someone guess. The BA's requirements say you confirmed round-half-up, and their criteria test that it rounds 2.345 to 2.35 and 2.335 to 2.34. I can't see the confirmation itself in the run files: the issue has no comments, so it presumably came through the resume answer. The design states that the mode comes from your confirmation and that the Developer must not change it. If that confirmation never actually happened, the run should go back to you. The fix would be a one-line change of the rounding setting.\n\n**Design in short:**\n- **Interface:** `round_currency(amount: int | float | Decimal) -> Decimal`. It uses only the standard `decimal` module, adds no dependencies, and leaves `health_check` and `add` untouched.\n- **Converting the input:** floats go through `str()` first, so 2.345 is read as `'2.345'`. Converting the float directly gives `2.34499…`, which would round down to 2.34 and fail AC-3, AC-4, AC-6 and AC-8. Integers and Decimals are converted directly.\n- **Rounding:** it rounds to 2 places with half-up passed explicitly, so ties go away from zero (-2.345 becomes -2.35). It doesn't touch or rely on Python's global decimal settings, which default to banker's rounding.\n- **Return type:** it returns a `Decimal`, not a float, so values stay exact and keep trailing zeros (5 becomes `5.00`). The tradeoff is that callers who want a float have to convert it themselves.\n- **Out of scope:** None, strings, NaN, infinity, negative zero (-0.001 gives `-0.00`) and very large values get no special handling.\n\nThere are 12 constraints, each tied to a criterion or a design decision. They include that the Developer only edits `src/` and QA writes the tests.\n\nFiles are in `.dark-factory/runs/b39a98a9-9a3f-4d78-9b7c-2601b823d6a5/artifacts/ARCHITECTURE/`:\n- `design.md`\n- `constraints.json`","ttft_ms":3767,"type":"result","duration_ms":70463,"uuid":"34654936-c33e-4369-b82a-263c7319349f","ttft_stream_ms":2915,"time_to_request_ms":140,"first_content_frame_ms":2915,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
